{% column %}
*Drum Sing Dance*

## Drumming, singing & dance in one action-packed session

Students learn funky drumming and dance breaks based on traditional West African rhythms. First, they alternate between drums, dance and traditional songs. Then everyone comes together as one ensemble. Because it’s fast-paced and adaptable, it suits large groups and every age level.

### Traditional rhythms from Guinea, Mali & Ghana

Rather than simply learning a collection of beats, students discover the foundations of traditional rhythms. They also hear the stories and cultural significance behind them, shared by artists who learned them first-hand.

{% button label="Book Drum Sing Dance" href="/quick-quote/" align="left" /%}
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}
{% /section %}

*For teachers*

## How our school incursions link to the curriculum

Rather than being passive observers, students become part of the learning. For example, they explore rhythmic patterns, tempo and musical structure, respond to cues and create group performances. As a result, our school incursions complement The Arts: Music and Dance, as well as the General Capabilities in the [Australian Curriculum](https://v9.australiancurriculum.edu.au/).

{% columns layout="equal" align="top" %}
{% column %}
### Music & The Arts

Students explore rhythm, tempo, repetition, patterns and dynamics. They also build listening skills and discover how individual parts combine into an ensemble.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
### Intercultural Understanding

Students experience the rhythms, instruments, songs and dance of West Africa. In addition, they learn about the music’s role in community and identity.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
### Personal & Social Capability

Students listen to one another, take turns and follow musical direction, so everyone contributes to a shared outcome.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
### Movement, Creativity & Expression

Students develop coordination, body awareness and confidence. They also learn to use movement as communication and artistic expression.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

### Extending learning beyond the workshop

Teachers can also build on the incursion afterwards, for example through composition, rhythm exercises, dance or cultural studies. In addition, we welcome conversations with teachers and curriculum coordinators about your learning objectives.

